Interpretation of negative Sobol indices (applies to x_target results)
Sobol’ first‑order indices S_1 are defined as non‑negative fractions of output variance attributable to the main (non‑interaction) effect of each input; therefore, the true indices satisfy 0≤S1≤1. In practice, Sobol indices are estimated from a finite Sobol‑QMC sample using Monte‑Carlo/QMC estimators (here, Jansen/Saltelli‑type estimators (Saltelli et al., 2010)). Finite‑sample estimation error can yield slightly negative S_1 values when the true first‑order effect is close to zero, because these estimators are noisy and are not constrained to [0,1] for finite n.  In this study, the parameters with negative S_1(q_EUR and b) also exhibit very small total‑order effects (S_T), indicating that their overall contribution to output variance (including interactions) is minor; therefore, the negative S_1 estimates are interpreted as approximately zero within sampling uncertainty. Accordingly, parameter importance is reported primarily using total‑order indices S_T, while negative first‑order estimates are treated as estimation artefacts rather than evidence of “negative sensitivity”.
Increasing the base sample size n (e.g., from 2048 to 8192) typically reduces estimator variance and thus makes such artefacts less likely, but this refinement is outside the current research scope. Future work may report bootstrap confidence intervals for S_1 and S_T to quantify estimator uncertainty for avoid negative Sobol indices for x_target.
